# Layout

A domain is a vertical slice.
One folder holds the user's word for a capability, its renderer layers, and the main-process code that serves it.
The folder name is the user's word, never a technology word.

```
apps/malini/src/
  main/        node bootstrap only: index.ts, window.ts, context.ts, events.ts, errors.ts, modules.ts,
               ipc/registry.ts, db/, git/, docker/, process/, fs/                  alias $main (node only)
  preload/     index.ts, index.d.ts: window.malini, typed invoke and on from $contract
  renderer/    index.html, public/, src/ (main.ts, app.css, styles/, vite-env.d.ts)
  contract/    commands.ts, events.ts, ipc.ts, wire types, protocol-contract.generated.ts  alias $contract
  lib/                                                                              alias $lib
    app/               the consumer domain: shell, sidebar, layouts, pages, routes.ts, boot
    chat/              transcript and lead timeline, composer, prompt queue, sessions and runs, approvals, checkpoints, deliveries, lead decisions
    pull-requests/     pull request state, actions, top-bar status, merge
    jobs/              jobs, their triggers and Thens, job runs and their runner, suggestions, Job center
    skills/            skill usage, turning skills off in Claude settings, the skills a job loads
    extensions/        runtime, host adapters, inspector, directory, configuration, opened files
    browser/           browser tabs in the inspector, the agent's browser tools
    stats/             sidebar resource readout, stats panel, what keeps running, share cards, public profile
    scratchpad/        a repository's scratchpad, its ideas move into new workstreams
    shared/                                                                         alias $shared
      repositories/    SHARED DOMAIN: repositories, clones, workstreams and their lifecycle
      providers/       SHARED DOMAIN: model catalog, model ids, provider auth, capabilities
      sounds/          SHARED DOMAIN: the sounds Malini plays, your picks, your own recordings
      port/            typed invoke(), onPlatformEvent()
      router/          hash router, current page, last route, history ledger, hrefs
      extensions/      the extension seams other domains touch
      auth/ env/ errors/ performance/ shell/ storage/ system/
```

Every domain, including the two shared ones:

```
<domain>/
  <domain>.api.ts        the only file other domains import (renderer side)
  <domain>.platform.ts   the only file other domains' platform/ imports (node side)
  domain/                pure TS shared by BOTH processes; no svelte, no node, no electron
  application/           renderer: commands/, queries/, hooks/
  infrastructure/        renderer: stores/, aggregates/, services/, mappers/
  presentation/          renderer: .svelte and pure view helpers
  platform/              main process: register.ts, services, *.repository.ts
```

`app` has no `app.api.ts`: nothing imports it.
`src/renderer/src/main.ts` mounts `$lib/app/presentation/App.svelte`.

Rules:

- No `index.ts` anywhere under `src/lib` or `src/contract`.
  `src/main/index.ts` and `src/preload/index.ts` are entry points, not barrels.
- No `export *`, anywhere.
- No relative import that leaves the domain folder.
  Cross-domain goes through an alias and an api file.
- `domain/` is type-checked by both the web and the node project.
  A domain file that touches `Buffer` or `document` fails one of them, which is the point.
- Aliases: `$main` (node only), `$lib` (`src/lib`), `$shared` (`src/lib/shared`), `$contract` (`src/contract`), `$hyper-ui`.
- `$shared/port` imports only `$contract`, its own folder, and packages.
  Infrastructure, flat `$shared/<util>` modules and the renderer entry may import it; domain, application, presentation and api files may not.

`@malini/desktop/domain-boundaries` enforces this at `error` on all of `src/**` and `tests/**`.
Test files, fixtures and generated files are exempt.
The per-layer rules name the check that reports each violation.
